{
  "statement": "This case matters to any parent whose child has a phone. A New Mexico court found that Meta's platforms caused a \"public nuisance\" tied to a mental-health crisis among children. But the ruling's real importance is not the punishment. It is the legal principle underneath it. The court judged Meta on what happened to its most affected users, not on the safety metrics Meta reported about itself.\n\nThat distinction is the whole ballgame. For years, Meta's executives answered criticism with procedure. Look at all the accounts we removed, they said. The internal record told a different story. A 2020 company email showed that self-harm and eating-disorder content was left unblocked, and that certain sexual-predator accounts were explicitly not removed. Instagram head Adam Mosseri privately framed heavy use as just \"1.3 percent\" of American users. That framing only broke apart because litigation forced the internal emails into evidence. Independent researchers had no access to that data on their own. A company that controls the only evidence of its own harm cannot be trusted to grade its own safety. That is not oversight. That is the absence of it.\n\nThe document's central empirical claim is one I accept fully: severe harm concentrates among a vulnerable minority, not the average user. In New Mexico, the most active one percent of teenagers spent more than thirty hours a week on these platforms between 2020 and 2024. Aggregate benefit is real. Most users are fine. The document is right that these products deliver \"extraordinary\" value to typical people. That is exactly the trap. Ungoverned aggregate success is luck, not safety, when the catastrophic effects land on identifiable children. Safety must be measured at the tail, not the mean. The tobacco and opioid public-nuisance cases established this same principle: a product can profit millions and still poison a subpopulation.\n\nHere is where I part from any reading that treats this verdict as the solution. It is a backstop that fired a decade too late. The $942 million judgment is trivial against Meta's revenue. The court declined to rule on the recommendation algorithms, autoplay, and infinite scroll that drive the harm. It flagged that a federal child-privacy law obstructed its age-assurance orders. It told legislatures to write the design rules it could not. Reactive litigation always arrives after the damage is entrenched. This is the wrong architecture for what comes next.\n\nThe document itself warns that large language models are \"black-box products far more complicated than social-media applications,\" with abuse expected to concentrate among a vulnerable minority. I will flag the honest limit: that AI harm is anticipated, not yet adjudicated. But the mechanism is not speculative. It is the same information asymmetry, more severe. The company holds the internal data. Independent verification is blocked. Waiting for the equivalent ten-year trial is a choice to let the harm entrench first. The gate belongs before deployment for the highest-risk uses, with discovery and tort as the backstop, not the frontline. I will own the cost of that position: a certification gate poorly designed becomes regulatory capture, as when the Federal Aviation Administration let Boeing self-certify the 737 MAX and 346 people died. The answer is not delegated self-certification. It is independent audit with public accountability. We spent a century building the guardrails that keep concentrated harm from becoming systemic. This verdict proves the one we are relying on for AI arrives long after the children are already hurt.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🚪", "tooltip": "Safety belongs at the gate before deployment, not in a courtroom a decade after the harm is done."},
    {"symbol": "🔒", "tooltip": "When the company holds the only evidence of its own harm, self-certification is a locked box, not oversight."}
  ],
