    {"claim": "The court judged Meta on what happened to its most affected users, not on the safety metrics Meta reported about itself.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A company that controls the only evidence of its own harm cannot be trusted to grade its own safety.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A 2020 company email showed self-harm and eating-disorder content left unblocked, and certain sexual-predator accounts explicitly not removed.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The most active one percent of New Mexico teenagers spent more than thirty hours a week on these platforms between 2020 and 2024.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Ungoverned aggregate success is luck, not safety, when the catastrophic effects land on identifiable children.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The $942 million judgment is trivial against Meta's revenue, and the court declined to rule on the algorithms driving the harm.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The gate belongs before deployment for the highest-risk uses, with discovery and tort as the backstop, not the frontline.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "A certification gate poorly designed becomes regulatory capture, as when the FAA let Boeing self-certify the 737 MAX and 346 people died.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
