rsion of it.\n\nSafetyist's deepest point is the one I most agree with: discovery only worked because internal emails surfaced, because the company controls the only evidence of its own harm. That is true. And it indicts the gate too. A pre-launch gate runs on the firm's pre-launch data, which is the same monopolized evidence, just earlier and thinner. The answer is to break the monopoly, not to move it forward in time. Open weights and decentralized telemetry mean harm surfaces continuously, in production, visible to outside auditors, not sealed in a legal vault for a decade until a lawsuit pries it loose. That is capability leadership through open accountability, and it attacks the exact asymmetry Safetyist diagnosed without blocking a single launch.\n\nI will state my losing condition plainly. An irreversible, deployment-scale harm that live monitoring demonstrably failed to catch in time counts against me. So I bound it: independent post-deployment audit, symmetric liability that bites the firm and its vendors alike, and real-time telemetry aimed straight at the tail Safetyist correctly identified. For harms that propagate irreversibly to children, carve out a distribution-stage stop. That is not an average. That is a scalpel at the tail. But hear the cost of the gate one more time. Every month a treatment or a tutor sits in a certification queue, someone waits who did not have to. The 49% of students who say AI already improved their experience did not get that from a launch that was delayed eighteen months for paperwork. Who grades the queue's body count?",
